    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense for the high-level fields, but I'm worried about the granular level of the description. If I just use \"Unknown,\" it feels a bit too sterile for a collection that has such a strong individual \"character\" to the work, even if the name isn't there. \n\nIs there a standard way to use controlled vocabularies or specific descriptors to capture the *nature* of the ambiguity? For example, if the mark is truly illegible vs. just missing, is there a formal way to categorize that distinction in the metadata so future researchers understand the specific reason for the uncertainty?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In this case, you can leverage controlled vocabularies such as Simple Terms for Simple Descriptions (STS), which provides guidance on standardized vocabulary for capturing nuanced information about deterioration or degradation, including ambiguity in creator attribution. Alternatively, you could use a tailored description, such as \"Name indiscernible,\" \"Name potentially present but illegible,\" or \"Name absent but stylistic characteristics inconsistent with others by same creator.\" These descriptors allow you to convey the complexity of the attribution issue without resorting to a single \"Unknown\" term.",
      "source": "target",
