    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a helpful distinction—using \"indiscernible\" versus \"absent\" definitely adds the layer of precision I'm looking for. \n\nI'm also thinking about the edge cases where the attribution is almost \"mysterious.\" Like, when there are these very strange, almost deliberate marks that don't look like standard signatures or even accidental damage, and it almost feels like they were meant to be cryptic. In an archival setting, how do we handle these \"unidentifiable\" elements if they don't fit into a standard controlled vocabulary? Is there a way to acknowledge that a creator might have been intentionally obscure or even \"unseen\" without it sounding like we're just making things up?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To address these complex, enigmatic cases, you can utilize a DX ( Dublin Core Extensions) element such as dcmitypegesture, which allows for a more granular description of the attribute. However, since the Dublin Core metadata standard primarily focuses on the content creator, this retained metadata element provides an extension to add field depth when the creator attribute is ambiguous, unclear, or intentionally omitted.",
